  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA similar setup to Tuesday night is on tap for Wednesday night with \nhigh pressure parked overhead. Light to calm wind and clear skies \novernight will allow for efficient radiational cooling and fog \ndevelopment in river valleys. Like Tuesday night, don't think that \nit leaks out of the valleys very much, but cold surfaces and bridges \nwithin river valleys could become slick in the morning. Fog will \ndissipate after sunrise as temperatures begin to rise.\n\nThe expansive surface high pressure dominates through the rest of \nthe week and the first half of this coming weekend with a bone dry \ncolumn. This will allow for diurnal mixing to erode a few degrees \noff of dew points in the afternoons. Temperatures will gradually \nmoderate as well back into the 60s for most of the area by the end \nof the week when the surface high slides east and promotes southerly \ngradient flow. Probability for 60 degree highs reaches about \n60-80% south of Pittsburgh on Wednesday but then jumps to 70-90%\nareawide come Thursday and Friday. 70 degrees isn't even out of\nthe question on Saturday south of Pittsburgh where 30-50% \nprobabilities sneak in. We will continue to see some decent \ndiurnal temperature swings Tuesday-Thursday nights as light wind\nand clearing skies allow for efficient radiational cooling.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nOur next chance for rain is still on track to arrive late this \ncoming weekend as a trough arrives from the west. Previously \nmentioned uncertainty still hasn't quite resolved itself as we await \nthe parent trough to eject from the Rockies. Depth of the trough \nvaries with a deeper one introducing higher rain chances and a less \namplified solution with lower rain chances. Ensemble analysis \nstill suggests we get a clearer picture of the synoptic \nevolution by Friday with analysis of two upstream features: 1) \nthe parent trough ejecting out of the Rockies and 2) a shortwave\nsliding down through Manitoba. A weaker embedded shortwave on \nthe leading edge of the ejecting trough would lend to faster \nlongwave movement and lesser rain chance locally. A stronger \nembedded shortwave in Manitoba could then phase with the parent \nCONUS trough and lend to higher rain chance locally. Despite the\nvery dry antecedent atmosphere in place, PWATs will recover \nback to 0.75-1\", even in the \"drier\" solution. The chance for \nmeasurable rain rises to 40-60% areawide, but NBM 90th \npercentile, or the wet scenario, paints mainly 0.2-0.3\", so it \ndoesn't look to be much more than a wetting rain.",
